1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,432
You need a tool that is not on the server. Where does it come from?

2
00:00:04,452 --> 00:00:07,880
On Linux you do not download an installer from a website.

3
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:11,025
You ask a repository, and one command does the rest.

4
00:00:11,495 --> 00:00:12,414
In this video:

5
00:00:12,434 --> 00:00:17,443
install and update software packages from Red Hat Content Delivery Network,

6
00:00:17,463 --> 00:00:20,862
a remote repository, or from the local file system.

7
00:00:26,382 --> 00:00:28,965
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

8
00:00:29,435 --> 00:00:34,431
Install and update software packages from Red Hat Content Delivery Network,

9
00:00:34,451 --> 00:00:37,841
a remote repository, or from the local file system.

10
00:00:41,311 --> 00:00:46,411
We'll install and update from a remote repository, then install from a file on disk.

11
00:00:52,931 --> 00:00:57,699
Software comes as R P M packages, and D N F installs them.

12
00:00:57,719 --> 00:01:00,835
D N F gets packages from repositories,

13
00:01:00,855 --> 00:01:05,540
listed in files in slash E T C slash yum dot repos dot d.

14
00:01:11,010 --> 00:01:16,259
On real RHEL, the main repositories live on the Red Hat Content Delivery Network,

15
00:01:16,279 --> 00:01:21,072
and a system gets access after it is registered with subscription manager.

16
00:01:21,092 --> 00:01:26,016
Our lab is Rocky Linux and has no subscription, so we cannot show that step.

17
00:01:26,036 --> 00:01:30,894
Rocky has its own public repositories, and the D N F commands are the same.

18
00:01:39,364 --> 00:01:44,278
Before the demo, silently, the lab installs the D N F plugins package,

19
00:01:44,298 --> 00:01:48,366
which provides dnf download, and removes the tree package.

20
00:01:48,386 --> 00:01:50,480
These steps change the system.

21
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,287
First, which repositories are enabled? Dnf repolist lists them: an ID and a name.

22
00:02:05,307 --> 00:02:08,324
Here: app stream, base O S, and extras.

23
00:02:17,844 --> 00:02:22,420
Install from a remote repository: sudo dnf install dash y tree.

24
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:24,973
Dash y answers yes to the question.

25
00:02:24,993 --> 00:02:30,299
D N F lists what it will install and from which repository, downloads it,

26
00:02:30,319 --> 00:02:34,165
and installs it. We trimmed the middle of the output.

27
00:02:43,635 --> 00:02:48,885
Rpm dash q asks: is this package installed? It prints the exact version.

28
00:02:55,405 --> 00:02:59,488
Update with dnf upgrade, and a package name. Nothing to do:

29
00:02:59,508 --> 00:03:01,921
tree is already the newest version.

30
00:03:01,941 --> 00:03:06,372
Without a name, dnf upgrade updates every package on the system.

31
00:03:15,892 --> 00:03:22,143
Third source: a file on disk. First we remove tree with dnf remove, to start clean.

32
00:03:22,163 --> 00:03:28,112
Then dnf download tree saves the R P M file in the current folder. Ls lists it:

33
00:03:28,132 --> 00:03:30,379
the star means any characters.

34
00:03:41,899 --> 00:03:47,877
Now install from that file. Dnf install, then dot slash, which means: this folder.

35
00:03:47,897 --> 00:03:53,362
Dot slash tree star dot R P M. The repository column says: at command line.

36
00:03:53,382 --> 00:03:57,312
The package came from the file, not from a repository.

37
00:03:57,332 --> 00:04:00,823
D N F still checks and installs it the same way.

38
00:04:12,343 --> 00:04:15,779
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

39
00:04:17,299 --> 00:04:22,036
We reboot. The package is still installed: packages stay until you remove them.

40
00:04:29,556 --> 00:04:32,587
Your turn. Install tree from the repository.

41
00:04:32,607 --> 00:04:38,133
Download the l s o f R P M into your home folder, and install it from that file.

42
00:04:38,153 --> 00:04:39,243
Run the checker.

43
00:04:47,713 --> 00:04:49,463
Try before you open the solution.

44
00:04:54,983 --> 00:04:57,459
Next up: modify the system bootloader.

45
00:04:57,929 --> 00:05:00,832
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
